<interrogationes unitas="utf8">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I loop over the codepoints of a chorda?
    <responsum symbolum="utf8_decodere">Keep
      a cursor and an end pointer: p = s.datum, finis = s.datum +
      s.mensura, then while (p &lt; finis) { runa = utf8_decodere(&amp;p,
      finis); ... }. Inside that loop utf8_decodere always moves the
      cursor forward, by at least one byte even on bad input, so the
      loop ends (it moves nothing only when p is NIHIL or already at
      finis). It returns -1 (an s32) for invalid input; check runa
      &lt; 0 and decide whether to skip it or draw U+FFFD.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what does utf8_decodere reject, and how far does it skip on an error?
    <responsum symbolum="utf8_decodere utf8_longitudo_byte">It
      returns -1 for stray continuation bytes, the lead bytes F8-FF, a
      sequence cut off by finis, a lead byte not followed by enough
      continuation bytes, overlong forms (including the C0 and C1
      leads), surrogates (U+D800-DFFF) and values above U+10FFFF. The
      skip is not uniform: a bad lead, a truncated sequence or a
      missing continuation byte advances one byte, but an overlong
      form, a surrogate or a value too large advances over the whole
      sequence (II-IV bytes).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I encode a codepoint into UTF-8 bytes?
    <responsum symbolum="utf8_codere">utf8_codere(runa,
      buffer) writes one to four bytes and returns how many; give it a
      buffer of at least IV bytes. It returns 0 and writes nothing for
      a NIHIL buffer, negative values, surrogates (U+D800-DFFF) and
      values above U+10FFFF. It does not NUL-terminate.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I use utf8_numerare_runas to work out how wide text is on screen?
    <responsum symbolum="utf8_numerare_runas runae_latitudo runae_latitudo_textus">No:
      it counts codepoints, not terminal cells. A CJK character is one
      codepoint but two cells, and a combining accent is a codepoint of
      width zero. Use runae_latitudo for one codepoint and
      runae_latitudo_textus (which walks grapheme clusters under a
      RunaePolitica) for a run of text; both come from lib/runae.c,
      built from fixed Unicode tables. Invalid input also counts
      unevenly, whatever the header says: each step of utf8_decodere
      counts as one, so a three-byte overlong form counts once but a
      sequence cut short counts once per byte.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I use utf8_proxima_runa and utf8_decodere interchangeably to step through text?
    <responsum symbolum="utf8_proxima_runa utf8_decodere utf8_prior_runa">Only
      on valid UTF-8. utf8_proxima_runa looks at the lead byte alone
      and jumps its full length without checking the continuation
      bytes, so on the bytes E2 'A' 'B' it skips straight past both
      letters, while utf8_decodere returns -1 and advances one byte to
      'A'. utf8_prior_runa steps back over at most three continuation
      bytes and validates nothing either. Use utf8_decodere when the
      input may be broken.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why do the utf8 functions take i8 pointers instead of char?
    <responsum symbolum="utf8_decodere utf8_longitudo_byte chorda" via="include/latina.h">i8
      is unsigned char in the house types and chorda.datum is i8*, so
      bytes 0x80-0xFF compare and shift as positive values and a
      chorda can be passed in without casts. The whole library is pure
      functions over a byte range: no allocation, no piscina, nothing
      outside latina.h, so it is cheap to use anywhere.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
